  "body_html": "<h1>Unilateral Deviation</h1>\n<p>Given a profile $s\\in S$, a player $i$, and an alternative strategy\n$t_i\\in S_i$, the unilateral deviation of player $i$ replaces only player\n$i$'s component:\n$$\n  (t_i,s_{-i})_j=\n  \\begin{cases}\n    t_i, &amp; j=i,\\\\\n    s_j, &amp; j\\ne i.\n  \\end{cases}\n$$\nThis is the basic local move used in best-response, dominance, and Nash\nequilibrium conditions.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[MFoGT, Section 1.3.4] Laraki, Renault, and Sorin, <em>Mathematical Foundations of Game Theory</em>, <code>references/MathFoundationsGameTheory.pdf</code>. Nash equilibrium is stated by absence of profitable unilateral deviations.</li>\n<li>[MFoGT, Def. 4.5.1] Laraki, Renault, and Sorin, <em>Mathematical Foundations of Game Theory</em>, <code>references/MathFoundationsGameTheory.pdf</code>. Deviation t_i against s_{-i}.</li>\n</ul>",
